import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.G0y0PQA7.js";const r="/relio/shots/en/ddl-options.png",u=JSON.parse('{"title":"DDL","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/features/ddl.md","filePath":"en/features/ddl.md"}'),s={name:"en/features/ddl.md"};function i(d,e,l,h,c,p){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="ddl" tabindex="-1">DDL <a class="header-anchor" href="#ddl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;DDL&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The fields of the DDL export dialog, which generates the SQL (DDL) to run on a database from your model, and how syntax and warnings depend on the database version.</p><p>The short version is in <a href="/relio/en/quick/export">Generate and export DDL</a> in Quick start.</p><h2 id="where-to-open-it" tabindex="-1">Where to open it <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-to-open-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where to open it&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>File › Export › DDL…</li><li>Canvas right-click › Document › Export › DDL…</li><li>Command line <code>relio export ddl</code> — <a href="/relio/en/features/cli#relio-export">Command line</a></li></ul><p>Supported databases: PostgreSQL · MySQL · MariaDB · Oracle · SQL Server · Db2 · H2 · HSQLDB · SQLite · Informix · MS Access · standard SQL.</p><h2 id="top-of-the-dialog-target-and-output" tabindex="-1">Top of the dialog: target and output <a class="header-anchor" href="#top-of-the-dialog-target-and-output" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Top of the dialog: target and output&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Database · Target product · Target version</td><td>The database and version to generate the DDL for. The preview updates as soon as you choose. The target version is remembered per file on this PC, not in the <code>.erm</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>Environment</td><td>The tablespace environment (Tablespace environments in Model › Options…). Tablespace attributes use this environment&#39;s values.</td></tr><tr><td>Category</td><td>Narrow the scope to <strong>All</strong> or a single category.</td></tr><tr><td>Output file</td><td>Where to save. Pick it with <strong>Browse…</strong>. If the file exists, Relio asks before overwriting.</td></tr><tr><td>Encoding · Line break</td><td>The character encoding and line ending of the file.</td></tr><tr><td>Open the file after saving</td><td>Opens the file once it is saved.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If you are only previewing another version, the dialog says &quot;Saving changes this document&#39;s target version&quot;. If you just preview and close, the target version stays as it was.</p><h2 id="statements-to-generate" tabindex="-1">Statements to generate <a class="header-anchor" href="#statements-to-generate" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Statements to generate&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Turn each kind of statement on or off. <strong>Turn all on</strong> turns everything on, and <strong>Turn off drop statements</strong> turns off only the drop statements.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Items</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Drop</td><td>Drop indexes · Drop views · Drop triggers · Drop tables · Drop sequences · Drop tablespaces</td></tr><tr><td>Create</td><td>Create tablespaces · Create sequences · Create tables · Create foreign keys · Create triggers · Create views · Create indexes</td></tr></tbody></table><p>On a fresh database, drop statements can fail with &quot;does not exist&quot; errors. Databases with an existence check syntax use it; for the others, a note is added at the top of the script.</p><h2 id="comments" tabindex="-1">Comments <a class="header-anchor" href="#comments" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Comments&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Generate DB comments (COMMENT etc.)</td><td>Turns table and column comments into database comment statements.</td></tr><tr><td>SQL comment line before objects (-- comment)</td><td>Adds a <code>--</code> comment line before tables and other objects.</td></tr><tr><td>SQL comment line before columns</td><td>Adds comment lines before columns too.</td></tr><tr><td>Comment value</td><td>What goes into the comment — <strong>Description</strong> · <strong>Logical name</strong> · <strong>Logical name : description</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Replace line breaks in comment values</td><td>When on, line breaks inside comments are replaced with the <strong>Replacement</strong> text. Leave it empty to remove the line breaks.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="options" tabindex="-1">Options <a class="header-anchor" href="#options" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Options&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Create with IF NOT EXISTS</td><td>Applies to tables, indexes and sequences only. On databases without this syntax, the statement is wrapped in an existence check or a warning is shown. To make the script fully re-runnable, use it together with drop statements.</td></tr><tr><td>Leave out schema prefixes</td><td>Objects are created in the connected user&#39;s default schema. If the same name exists in another schema, only that name keeps its prefix.</td></tr><tr><td>Use Oracle native types (boolean)</td><td>On Oracle 23 or later, true/false columns are created as <code>boolean</code> instead of <code>number(1,0)</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>Add SET SQLBLANKLINES ON when comments contain blank lines</td><td>Added at the very beginning of the script only when comments contain blank lines, so SQL*Plus does not end a statement at a blank line. When off, it is not added and a warning is shown.</td></tr><tr><td>Also drop tablespaces</td><td>Adds <code>DROP TABLESPACE</code> to the drop statements. This can delete data of other schemas in that tablespace. Drop tablespaces under Statements to generate must also be on.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>When you click <strong>Save</strong>, the environment, category, output file, encoding, line break, Open the file after saving, and the Statements to generate and Comments settings are saved in the document (<code>.erm</code>) and used next time. The target product (MySQL or MariaDB), the target version and the items on the &quot;Options&quot; tab are remembered in this PC&#39;s per-file records instead of the <code>.erm</code>. If you only change settings and close without saving, neither is changed.</p><figure class="shot" data-scene="ddl-options"><img src="'+r+'" alt="DDL export dialog — Statements to generate, Comments and Options" loading="lazy"><figcaption>DDL export dialog — Statements to generate, Comments and Options</figcaption></figure><h2 id="preview-and-save" tabindex="-1">Preview and save <a class="header-anchor" href="#preview-and-save" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Preview and save&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The <strong>Preview</strong> in the dialog shows the SQL that will be generated. For large output, line numbers and syntax coloring are turned off.</li><li><strong>Save</strong> writes the file. Click <strong>Open folder</strong> in the notice at the bottom right of the app window to open the folder in File Explorer with the file selected.</li><li>The result is reported like &quot;DDL saved (warnings: n — see the problem list)&quot;.</li></ul><h2 id="version-specific-syntax-and-warnings" tabindex="-1">Version-specific syntax and warnings <a class="header-anchor" href="#version-specific-syntax-and-warnings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Version-specific syntax and warnings&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Syntax that the chosen database version cannot run is removed or changed, with a warning. The dialog shows the number of warnings, and they are also kept in the problem list.</li><li>Actions the database does not support, such as some ON UPDATE and ON DELETE actions on relationships, are changed to a supported action or left out. The relationship properties dialog tells you in advance.</li><li>Tables with a column that has no physical name or data type are left out, with the message &quot;n tables left out — a column has no physical name or data type&quot;. Double-click the list to see that table on the canvas.</li><li>If there are design check errors (problems that make the DDL fail when run), the dialog shows &quot;This document has n errors that make the DDL fail&quot;, and <strong>Show problem list</strong> takes you straight there. Ignored errors are not counted. Saving is not blocked.</li></ul><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Tip</p><p>If you do not know the version, you can leave it as &quot;version not set&quot;. The DDL then uses syntax available from the lowest supported version of that database, so it runs on most versions.</p></div><h2 id="applying-directly-to-a-database" tabindex="-1">Applying directly to a database <a class="header-anchor" href="#applying-directly-to-a-database" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Applying directly to a database&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Instead of running a DDL file yourself, you can create the model directly in a database with File › Export to DB…. To apply only the changes to an existing database, use <strong>Generate migration SQL</strong> in the comparison view. <a href="/relio/en/features/database">Databases</a> · <a href="/relio/en/features/git#generating-migration-sql">Git and merging › Generating migration SQL</a></p>',26)])])}const g=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
